WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:04.671
When Barack Obama was inaugurated in 2009,
the oath of office was administered

00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:07.257
by Supreme Court Chief Justice
John Roberts,

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:10.260
who is known for being a stickler
for correct grammar.

00:00:10.385 --> 00:00:14.597
So much so that he decided
to change the wording of the oath from,

00:00:14.806 --> 00:00:18.435
“I solemnly swear that I will faithfully
execute the office of President

00:00:18.435 --> 00:00:19.561
of the United States,”

00:00:19.561 --> 00:00:25.150
to, “that I will execute the office of
President to the United States faithfully.”

00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:27.277
That I will execute...

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:31.114
The grammar crime that Roberts believed
the Founding Fathers had committed

00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:34.993
was splitting a verb
phrase, “will execute,” with an adverb,

00:00:35.035 --> 00:00:37.620
“faithfully”.

00:00:38.955 --> 00:00:41.082
This is similar to the popular rule

00:00:41.082 --> 00:00:45.420
against not splitting infinitives
like “to eat,” “to run” or “to think.”

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:50.383
Which means Captain Kirk
should have said “to go boldly

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:53.803
where no man has gone before.”

00:00:54.637 --> 00:00:56.765
Doesn't quite have the same ring, does it?

00:00:56.765 --> 00:00:59.559
Turns out that
just unilaterally deciding

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:02.979
to change the wording of the Constitution
isn't a good idea.

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:06.524
Some legal scholars worried
that if the oath wasn't recited

00:01:06.524 --> 00:01:09.319
verbatim,
Obama might not actually be president.

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:13.323
Just to be safe, the two men repeated
the ceremony later as written.

00:01:13.656 --> 00:01:17.786
This is an extreme example of how being
a grammar cop can get you into trouble.

00:01:18.119 --> 00:01:22.040
But besides the social and cultural
downsides of trying to police how others

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:26.127
talk, there are some persuasive arguments
that many of the grammatical errors

00:01:26.127 --> 00:01:29.380
that are frequently called out
might not be errors at all.

00:01:29.798 --> 00:01:31.299
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky

00:01:31.299 --> 00:01:33.593
and it's time to boldly
break some grammar rules

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:35.512
on Otherwords.

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:48.441
Have you ever corrected someone's grammar
and gotten the response,

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:51.945
“Wow. Thank you so much for pointing out
my foolish mistake.

00:01:52.195 --> 00:01:54.697
Please let me know if I make any others
in the future.”

00:01:55.532 --> 00:01:56.991
Yeah, not likely.

00:01:56.991 --> 00:02:00.370
Being told that you speak wrong
is uniquely insulting

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.332
because it's so tied to perceptions
of class, education and intelligence.

00:02:04.707 --> 00:02:08.378
The wealthy and educated have good grammar
and the poor and uneducated

00:02:08.378 --> 00:02:09.629
have bad grammar.

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:13.299
Believing in good grammar
is inherently prescriptive.

00:02:13.591 --> 00:02:15.635
It's concerned
with how things ought to be.

00:02:15.927 --> 00:02:19.305
But linguistics,
like most sciences, is descriptive.

00:02:19.597 --> 00:02:22.600
It tries to describe the way things
actually are.

00:02:23.184 --> 00:02:25.770
Just as it would be ludicrous
for a biologist to say

00:02:25.854 --> 00:02:28.940
that a species of bird doesn't fly
the way it ought to,

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:32.861
it's equally nonsensical for a linguist
to say that certain groups of people

00:02:32.861 --> 00:02:37.323
don't talk the way they should. Instead of
correct speech versus incorrect speech,

00:02:37.574 --> 00:02:41.452
what linguists observe in the real world
is a bunch of different dialects

00:02:41.452 --> 00:02:43.163
with different sets of rules.

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:46.958
These rules can shift or change,
but they're always internally consistent.

00:02:47.375 --> 00:02:50.712
If a group of teenagers, for example,
were just speaking a degraded

00:02:50.712 --> 00:02:54.465
or sloppy form of English, then
it should be easy for an adult to imitate.

00:02:54.799 --> 00:02:56.676
Of course we know it isn't.

00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:59.929
Their dialect has rules and they know
instantly when they're broken.

00:03:00.096 --> 00:03:04.267
Perhaps the most denigrated of dialects
is African-American English

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:05.810
or Black English.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:09.814
Many features of the dialect,
like negative concord and zero copula

00:03:09.981 --> 00:03:13.776
are common grammatical practices
across many languages.

00:03:14.152 --> 00:03:18.156
Yet some continue to use them
as examples of bad grammar.

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:19.949
Throughout much of the 20th century,

00:03:19.949 --> 00:03:23.745
many educational psychologists
thought that low income black kids

00:03:23.745 --> 00:03:27.540
spoke a stunted or inferior
version of English, and some believed

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.542
that they had virtually no language
at all.

00:03:29.918 --> 00:03:33.630
Then, in the late sixties,
a linguistic study of children in Harlem

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:37.467
found that the grammar they used was,
in fact, rich and consistent, capable

00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:40.970
of just as much nuance and precision
as any other English dialect.

00:03:41.179 --> 00:03:45.308
This was an important step in academics
acknowledging that the disproportionately

00:03:45.308 --> 00:03:49.229
poor school performance of Black
children was due in part to the fact that,

00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:52.440
unlike their white, middle class
counterparts, they were being asked

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.485
to learn in a different dialect
than the one they were raised in.

00:03:55.777 --> 00:03:58.780
The study also found that when compared
with upper middle class

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:03.368
speakers of so-called
Standard English, speakers of AAE tended

00:04:03.368 --> 00:04:07.956
to commit fewer grammatical mistakes,
mismatches and redundancies. Why?

00:04:08.373 --> 00:04:12.460
Possibly because what we call Standard
English doesn't come very naturally.

00:04:12.835 --> 00:04:16.089
It contains a lot of arbitrary rules
made up hundreds of years ago,

00:04:16.297 --> 00:04:19.425
which most of us
have had to learn by force in school

00:04:19.509 --> 00:04:21.678
rather than naturally with friends
and family.

00:04:21.678 --> 00:04:25.181
Take Captain Kirk’s supposed mistake:
the split infinitive.

00:04:25.473 --> 00:04:28.351
The foundation for this rule dates
back to the 19th century

00:04:28.393 --> 00:04:31.396
suggestion by the scholar Henry
Alford:

00:04:31.688 --> 00:04:34.565
The safest choice
is to avoid splitting infinitives.

00:04:35.149 --> 00:04:37.944
Somehow, this morphed into a hard rule

00:04:37.944 --> 00:04:41.197
by the middle of the 20th century,
with some arguing that since

00:04:41.197 --> 00:04:44.075
Latin never splits infinitives,
neither should English.

00:04:44.325 --> 00:04:48.788
But you can't split Latin infinitives
because they're always just one word,

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:50.123
like amare.

00:04:50.123 --> 00:04:52.125
English infinitives are two words,

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:54.544
and there's no good reason
why you can't split them.

00:04:54.669 --> 00:04:58.923
Another supposed rule based on
Latin conventions is not ending a sentence

00:04:58.923 --> 00:05:02.468
with a preposition, like that infamous
hack Shakespeare did when he wrote

00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:05.430
We are such stuff as dreams are made on...

00:05:07.682 --> 00:05:09.017
Again, in Latin,

00:05:09.017 --> 00:05:11.894
prepositions can't be separated.

00:05:11.894 --> 00:05:14.230
But that's no reason why we can't do it
in English.

00:05:14.564 --> 00:05:17.900
And would anyone really say, “To
whom do you think you're talking?”

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:20.194
instead of
“Who do you think you're talking to?”

00:05:20.194 --> 00:05:25.366
Speaking of whom, it is technically
the objective case for the nominative who.

00:05:25.742 --> 00:05:28.619
But over the last century,
it's largely fallen out of usage,

00:05:28.619 --> 00:05:30.621
and that's hardly unheard of.

00:05:30.621 --> 00:05:34.250
Plenty of other pronouns
use one word for subject and object.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:36.794
Like it, that, what, and where.

00:05:37.211 --> 00:05:39.088
Until relatively recently,

00:05:39.088 --> 00:05:42.592
there were four second person pronouns
that all collapsed into you.

00:05:43.092 --> 00:05:47.597
It seems pretty clear that whom will
someday be as archaic as thee and thou.

00:05:47.847 --> 00:05:52.185
Okay, so what about that most offensive
of grammatical transgressions?

00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:54.395
Me and Jennifer are going swimming.

00:05:54.395 --> 00:05:56.773
We can all agree that's wrong, right?

00:05:56.773 --> 00:05:59.317
After all,
you wouldn't say “Me is going swimming.”

00:05:59.984 --> 00:06:04.238
Believe it or not, some linguists
even think this construction isn't so bad.

00:06:04.822 --> 00:06:08.493
The head of a clause is the key word
that determines its nature.

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:14.165
So in this noun phrase, the head is man
and the verb must agree with it.

00:06:14.415 --> 00:06:18.836
The man is going swimming.
But conjunctive phrases,

00:06:18.878 --> 00:06:21.464
those with multiple nouns,
are considered headless.

00:06:21.839 --> 00:06:24.384
There is no one word that determines case.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:27.762
Neither noun on its own matches
the verb are.

00:06:27.762 --> 00:06:31.265
The individual parts are not required to
perfectly agree with the verb.

00:06:32.016 --> 00:06:34.852
Which of these two sentences
are you more likely to say?

00:06:35.353 --> 00:06:38.106
“Everyone but her won an award.”

00:06:38.106 --> 00:06:41.442
Or “Everyone but she won an award.”

00:06:41.442 --> 00:06:43.861
Even though the second is technically correct,

00:06:43.861 --> 00:06:47.198
the vast majority of English speakers
find it clunky and unnatural.

00:06:47.407 --> 00:06:48.699
Why is that?

00:06:48.699 --> 00:06:51.619
Linguist Joseph Emonds
argues that English speakers have

00:06:51.619 --> 00:06:56.541
a natural inclination to use the objective
pronoun case in conjunctive clauses.

00:06:57.125 --> 00:06:58.126
This is perhaps because

00:06:58.126 --> 00:07:01.629
we mentally reserve the nominative case
for subject heads only.

00:07:02.046 --> 00:07:05.508
It just doesn't feel right to say he
or she or they,

00:07:05.675 --> 00:07:08.428
unless it refers
to the sole subject of a sentence.

00:07:08.886 --> 00:07:12.014
This is a perfectly consistent
and reasonable grammatical rule.

00:07:12.306 --> 00:07:15.852
Yet we've had it beaten out of us
in favor of one that is no more logical

00:07:15.852 --> 00:07:17.437
and a lot less natural.

00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:19.230
If this isn't enough for you,

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:23.151
I am even going to defend the figurative
use of the word literally.

00:07:23.151 --> 00:07:25.486
As in, I am literally starving to death.

00:07:26.195 --> 00:07:29.449
This could be called
an ironic intensifier, like saying,

00:07:29.657 --> 00:07:31.826
Oh, I could definitely use
another problem today

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:34.745
and the usage isn't even new.

00:07:34.745 --> 00:07:39.459
In Nicholas Nickleby, Charles Dickens wrote:
“His looks were very haggard and his limbs

00:07:39.459 --> 00:07:42.462
and body
literally worn to the bone.”

00:07:42.795 --> 00:07:46.841
At this point, some of you may be literally gasping
for breath at these grammatical heresies.

00:07:47.216 --> 00:07:50.678
So let's say a few things
in defense of so-called Standard English.

00:07:51.095 --> 00:07:53.347
First of all,
it has allowed English grammar

00:07:53.347 --> 00:07:56.017
to stay remarkably unchanged
for hundreds of years.

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:59.937
That's why Jane Austen sounds more
or less modern to our ears.

00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:02.398
Yet Shakespeare
would have sounded archaic to her,

00:08:02.773 --> 00:08:05.526
even though the two time spans
are roughly equivalent.

00:08:05.735 --> 00:08:10.156
It's also useful in expressing formality.
If you're going to speak at a funeral

00:08:10.156 --> 00:08:11.407
or go on a job interview,

00:08:11.407 --> 00:08:15.286
using standard English as a social cue
that you take the occasion seriously.

00:08:15.286 --> 00:08:16.496
And like it or not,

00:08:16.496 --> 00:08:19.916
Standard English is still associated
with proficiency and education.

00:08:20.291 --> 00:08:23.377
Many career fields require
that you be able to speak and write it

00:08:23.377 --> 00:08:24.504
if you want to get far.

00:08:24.504 --> 00:08:27.465
Being proficient in Standard
English is a valuable asset, but

00:08:27.465 --> 00:08:30.134
it's not necessary for everyone
or every occasion.

00:08:30.635 --> 00:08:33.804
Expecting people to use a formal dialect
in casual situations

00:08:33.804 --> 00:08:36.641
like a party
or comment section is ludicrous.

00:08:37.016 --> 00:08:39.519
And if the point of language
is to facilitate understanding

00:08:39.644 --> 00:08:42.939
form social bonds, it's
almost always counterproductive

00:08:42.939 --> 00:08:45.316
to correct someone's grammar
if they're not asking for it.

00:08:45.816 --> 00:08:48.736
And maybe that's the main argument
for not being a grammar cop.

00:08:48.986 --> 00:08:49.987
No one likes it.

00:08:49.987 --> 00:08:52.949
Literally, no one.

00:08:54.867 --> 00:08:55.743
History lovers.

00:08:55.743 --> 00:08:59.163
We need to tell you about a new series
from PBS called

00:08:59.163 --> 00:09:02.833
The Bigger Picture, hosted by Professor
Vincent Brown.

00:09:03.042 --> 00:09:04.210
It's a show that examines

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:08.422
famous photographs and unpacks
the historical context around them.

00:09:08.464 --> 00:09:09.382
In doing so,

00:09:09.382 --> 00:09:10.800
we reveal what these photos

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:13.970
can tell us about our history,
but also how we view ourselves.

00:09:14.011 --> 00:09:17.056
Check the link in the description
and head to the PBS YouTube channel

00:09:17.056 --> 00:09:18.224
to see it for yourself.

00:09:18.224 --> 00:09:20.810
Tell them Otherwords sent you

00:09:21.185 --> 00:09:22.812
some legal scholars worried

00:09:22.812 --> 00:09:26.065
that if the oath wasn't
Oath, oaf I said, oaf, okay.

00:09:26.315 --> 00:09:28.693
But that's not no... that’s not

00:09:28.693 --> 00:09:32.446
no reason why. It's time to boldly break
some of gram--

00:09:32.572 --> 00:09:33.281
That was all weird.

00:09:33.281 --> 00:09:34.782
That was all sorts of not it.

